- context_read(from_block, to_block, page) prints blocks as text (all of them if you give no range). A result holds up to 8,000 characters, so a long view is split into pages.
- context_apply(ops) changes your context in one action, with any number of operations:
  - {"op": "replace_text", "block": n, "old": "...", "new": "..."} replaces text found exactly once in a block;
  - {"op": "replace_block", "block": n, "text": "..."} replaces all of a block's text;
  - {"op": "delete", "blocks": [n, ...]} removes blocks; a tool call and its result are removed as a pair;
  - {"op": "substitute", "pattern": "...", "replacement": "...", "blocks": [n, ...]} applies a regular expression to the listed blocks, or to every block if "blocks" is left out;
  - {"op": "insert", "after": n, "role": "notes", "text": "..."} adds a block after block n (0 means at the start), labelled with the role you give.
The operations of one call are checked together and take effect on your next call, all or none, and the call returns a short receipt. Block numbers are those of your context right now; they shift once an edit takes effect. The text of a tool call has to remain a JSON object. A response that only calls context_apply, with every edit accepted, does not count as a task step. The system prompt and the first task message cannot be edited.
